.Dd July 3, 2026
.Dt XTC_PKEY 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_pkey_supported ,
.Nm xtc_pkey_alloc ,
.Nm xtc_pkey_protect ,
.Nm xtc_pkey_set_access ,
.Nm xtc_pkey_free
.Nd memory-protection-key (PKU) tier for in-process isolation
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_pkey.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pkey_supported "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pkey_alloc "int *out_key"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pkey_protect "void *addr" "size_t len" "int key"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pkey_set_access "int key" "int allow_read" "int allow_write"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_pkey_free "int key"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_pkey
is an optional memory-protection-key tier for in-process memory
isolation, built on the hardware protection-key facility (Intel PKU /
the
.Xr pkey_alloc 2
family on Linux/x86 with OSPKE).  A protection key tags a range of
pages; the calling thread's per-key access rights then gate reads and
writes to those pages independently of the pages' base protection
bits, and rights can be flipped cheaply (a register write) without a
syscall.  This lets a thread temporarily seal off a region -- for
example to guard a metadata arena against stray writes outside a
short critical window.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pkey_supported
returns non-zero if the running kernel and CPU provide protection
keys.  All other functions return
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
where the facility is unavailable (non-glibc libc without the
.Fn pkey_*
wrappers, non-x86, macOS, Windows), so callers can probe once and
fall back.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pkey_alloc
allocates a protection key into
.Fa *out_key .
Keys are a scarce hardware resource (typically 15 usable); free them
with
.Fn xtc_pkey_free .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pkey_protect
tags the
.Fa len
bytes at
.Fa addr
(page-aligned range) with
.Fa key .
Access is thereafter gated by the key's per-thread rights.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pkey_set_access
sets the calling thread's rights for
.Fa key :
.Fa allow_read
and
.Fa allow_write
are booleans.  Disabling write, then writing a tagged page, faults
(delivers
.Dv SIGSEGV ) .
Rights are per-thread: each thread sets its own view.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_pkey_free
releases
.Fa key .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_pkey_supported
returns 1 if supported, 0 otherwise.  The
.Ft int
functions return
.Dv XTC_OK
on success, a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code on error, or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
where protection keys are unavailable.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pkey_alloc 2 ,
.Xr pkey_mprotect 2 ,
.Xr xtc_mctx 3
.Sh AUTHORS
.An The XTC Project .
